        "math_academy_id": "q-105554",
        "problem": "Given that $F=y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,i+{x}^{2}{cos}^{2}\u2061y\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$",
        "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\u2207\\cdot F=\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot F & =\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({x}^{2}{cos}^{2}\u2061y) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
